    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I've been seeing a lot of variation in how the soil reacts even after the compost goes down. Do you think the nitrogen's just not breaking down fast enough, or is there something else in the soil chemistry that might be locking it up from the plants?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Soil variation suggests that nutrient availability is likely the issue, even if total nitrogen is present. Nitrogen can become \"locked up\" through processes like nitrification, where certain soil microbes convert forms the plants can't absorb immediately. Another factor to consider is the availability of other co-nutrients, like magnesium or iron, which can cause deficiency symptoms mistaken for nitrogen lack. Testing your soil more comprehensively will help pinpoint if it's a breakdown rate issue or a chemical lockup.",
      "source": "target",
